  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Poles of a Transfer Function",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute poles of a rational transfer function by finding the zeros of its denominator polynomial and interpreting those s-plane values as the system-mode locations used in pole-zero analysis.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Poles of a Transfer Function",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Poles of a Transfer Function",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A transfer function is written as $H(s)=\\dfrac{N(s)}{D(s)}$, where $N(s)$ is the numerator polynomial and $D(s)$ is the denominator polynomial. Poles are defined from the denominator roots, while zeros come from numerator roots, so the two are distinguished by which polynomial is set to zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=\\dfrac{N(s)}{D(s)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This distinction is not cosmetic. If $D(s)=0$, the expression $H(s)$ has a denominator of zero, so the transfer-function value is not finite; that is why those roots are the poles and they mark singular points in symbolic form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "D(s)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the base case $H(s)=\\dfrac{1}{s+4}$. The denominator is $D(s)=s+4$. Set $s+4=0$, which gives the pole at $s=-4$. That single root is the pole value.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Next sections keep the same pole rule and add one variation at a time: first another linear denominator, then factored and expanded denominators, and then how pole locations are used in the $s$-plane interpretation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Poles of a Transfer Function",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Finding a Pole from a Linear Denominator Factor",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the poles of $H(s)=\\dfrac{3s+1}{s+5}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Identify the transfer function form first: in $H(s)=\\dfrac{3s+1}{s+5}$, the denominator is the piece that determines poles. So we focus on the denominator polynomial only.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=\\frac{3s+1}{s+5}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Step 1 is to read the denominator polynomial directly from the transfer function: the denominator is $D(s)=s+5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "D(s)=s+5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Step 2 applies the pole rule: set $D(s)=0$ and solve.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s+5=0 \\Rightarrow s=-5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Step 3 states the final pole result as a set: $\\{ -5 \\}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Poles of a Transfer Function",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding Poles from Factored Denominator Polynomials",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find all poles of $H(s)=\\\\frac{4}{(s+2)(s-3)}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The target cue is a factored denominator. For a transfer function, poles are exactly the values of $s$ that make the denominator zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=\\\\frac{4}{(s+2)(s-3)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "From this form, read the denominator as a product: $D(s)=(s+2)(s-3)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the pole rule for each factor: set the denominator equal to zero and use the zero-product rule to split into two equations.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "D(s)=0 \\\\Rightarrow (s+2)(s-3)=0 \\\\Rightarrow s+2=0 \\\\text{ or } s-3=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Solve each linear equation. From $s+2=0$, we get $s=-2$. From $s-3=0$, we get $s=3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "State the poles as a complete set: $\\\\{-2, 3\\\\}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Poles of a Transfer Function",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Finding Poles by Factoring an Expanded Denominator",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the poles of $H(s)=\\\\frac{2}{s^2+s-6}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This cue is an expanded denominator, so the first action is to identify the denominator polynomial: $D(s)=s^2+s-6$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=\\frac{2}{s^2+s-6}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a pole computation, set the denominator to zero and factor the quadratic before solving.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "D(s)=s^2+s-6=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Factor the denominator as $s^2+s-6=(s+3)(s-2)$. The zero-product rule then gives two equations.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(s+3)(s-2)=0 \\Rightarrow s+3=0 \\text{ or } s-2=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Solve each linear equation: $s=-3$ from $s+3=0$ and $s=2$ from $s-2=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Report the full pole set with both solutions: $\\{-3,2\\}$. This gives two poles, matching the quadratic degree.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Poles of a Transfer Function",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Reading Pole Values as Locations in the $s$-Plane",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "image-needed",
        "image_slots": [
          {
            "image_slot_id": "section-005-s-plane",
            "placement": "section",
            "location_placeholder": "[IMAGE-LOCATION:section-005]",
            "description_placeholder": "[IMAGE-DESCRIPTION:section-005]",
            "image_description": "An s-plane diagram with a horizontal real axis labeled Re(s), a vertical imaginary axis labeled Im(s), and two marked point locations at (-1,2) and (-1,-2) corresponding to the worked-example pole values, with no extra decorative elements."
          }
        ],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $H(s)=\\frac{2}{s^2+2s+5}$, find poles and state their $s$-plane coordinates.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a rational transfer function, so first identify the denominator and read the pole equation directly: $D(s)=s^2+2s+5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=\\frac{2}{s^2+2s+5}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the pole rule and set the denominator to zero: $s^2+2s+5=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "D(s)=s^2+2s+5=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Complete the square as $s^2+2s+5=(s+1)^2+4$, so $(s+1)^2=-4$ and $s+1=\\pm 2j$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s+1=\\pm 2j \\Rightarrow s=-1\\pm 2j",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Report the pole values as a pair: $\\{-1+2j, -1-2j\\}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Convert each pole with $s=\\sigma+j\\omega \\leftrightarrow (\\sigma,\\omega)$: first write the real part then the imaginary part. The poles map to $-1+2j\\mapsto(-1,2)$ and $-1-2j\\mapsto(-1,-2)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "-1+2j \\mapsto (-1,2),\\quad -1-2j \\mapsto (-1,-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "image",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": "section-005-s-plane"
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Both poles share the real coordinate $-1$, with imaginary coordinates that are opposite in sign, confirming the conjugate-pair coordinates expected from this denominator.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
